---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/guides/offer-discipline';
const title = 'Offer Discipline for SMS Campaigns | Shopify SMS Marketing';
const desc = 'Choose an incentive for a defined commercial problem, check the checkout behavior, and compare contribution against a credible alternative.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/guides" class="link text-sm">← All SMS guides</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Choose the smallest useful incentive</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Offer Discipline for SMS Campaigns</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">{desc}</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify">
<h2>State the problem before choosing the discount</h2>
<p>Clearing old sizes, introducing a new product and rewarding repeat customers are different jobs. Write the eligible products, available stock, target audience and desired behavior before selecting an incentive. Early access, a useful bundle or product education can be worth testing alongside a discount; none is guaranteed to beat a price reduction.</p>
<p>Past discount use is a clue, not proof that someone needs another discount. A customer may have redeemed a code that was offered to everyone. Likewise, high lifetime spend does not establish full-price intent. Use observed category interest and purchase history to form a hypothesis, then test it without assigning invented traits to an entire value quartile.</p>
<h2>Make the offer contract explicit</h2>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Record before launch</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Benefit</td><td>Percentage, fixed amount, shipping, gift or access; applicable products and minimum spend</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eligibility</td><td>Market, customer group, one-time or repeat use, exclusions and permission</td></tr>
<tr><td>Timing</td><td>Start, expiry and timezone; whether the landing page and checkout agree</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interactions</td><td>Automatic discounts, codes, subscriptions and shipping offers already active</td></tr>
<tr><td>Economics</td><td>Expected net receipts, product cost, fulfillment, support and channel cost</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/discounts/discount-combinations">Shopify documents discount classes, combination settings and eligibility limits</a>. Checkout behavior depends on the configured combinations and merchant eligibility. Checked November 25, 2026. Do not promise that a code stacks with every existing promotion.</p>
<h2>Rehearse the checkout paths</h2>
<p>Use a controlled cart with an eligible item, then one with an excluded item. Try the threshold boundary, an active automatic promotion, a returning customer and a shipping destination outside the intended market. Inspect the resulting total and error message. Include subscriptions if they are part of the offer; do not assume a one-time-product promotion behaves identically.</p>
<p>These are proposed checks, not a vendor test we conducted. Save the expected benefit and observed checkout total. If a public link can be forwarded, verify how eligibility is enforced before describing it as exclusive. An early-access link alone is not a stock reservation or a secure membership check.</p>
<h2>Calculate contribution without double counting</h2>
<p>Illustrative single-order worksheet: a $100 item discounted by $15 produces $85 in net sales before tax. With $40 product cost, $8 fulfillment and shipping subsidy, and $3 payment and variable service costs, contribution is $34 before campaign costs. The undiscounted equivalent is $49. The discount reduces contribution by $15; do not subtract that $15 again from the already discounted net sales.</p>
<p>If those assumptions held for every order, matching $490 contribution from ten undiscounted orders would require fifteen discounted orders: fourteen yield $476 and fifteen yield $510. This simplified comparison excludes returns, order mix changes and campaign costs. It is not a forecast that a discount will generate five extra orders. Model gifts and free shipping using their real incremental cost rather than their advertised retail value.</p>
<h2>Compare offers with a defensible counterfactual</h2>
<p>A proposed trial could randomize eligible subscribers between early access without a code and the same product announcement with a discount. Hold timing and audience rules consistent. If the question is whether any promotion helps, a no-message group answers a different question and may need its own allocation. Decide the comparison before sending.</p>
<p>Compare net contribution per eligible customer over a defined window, including returns and channel costs. Code redemptions and attributed revenue do not establish incrementality: some redeemers would have purchased anyway. Keep group membership stable and account for overlapping email or public sale exposure. Small groups, rare purchases or leaked codes can make the result inconclusive.</p>
<h2>Use scarcity and deadlines that can be honored</h2>
<p>Give a stock-based reminder an inventory owner and cancellation path. For a clearance, define what happens if the promoted size sells out while the message is queued. For a deadline, keep the message, landing page and checkout expiry consistent. Repeatedly extending a supposedly final offer changes the promise customers learn from the campaign.</p>
<p>Do not automatically escalate every non-buyer to a larger discount. Review whether stock, price, delivery terms or product fit prevented the purchase. Record which incentive you retain, for which audience, and why. Use <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> to decide whether the reminder deserves another touch and <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> to keep the offer consistent across email and text.</p>
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
